# On-Chain Swap Example in Rust (ICP)

The knowledge sources include a detailed ICRC-2 swap example, but it is written in **Motoko** (`motoko/icrc2-swap`). There is **no direct Rust equivalent** of the full swap canister in the provided knowledge sources. However, I can explain the architecture and key safety patterns clearly, and point you to the closest Rust resources available.

---

## Architecture

Three canisters are involved: [[ICRC-2 Swap README](https://github.com/dfinity/examples/blob/master/motoko/icrc2-swap/README.md)]

| Canister | Role |
|---|---|
| `token_a` | Standard ICRC-1/ICRC-2 ledger |
| `token_b` | Standard ICRC-1/ICRC-2 ledger |
| `backend` | Swap canister: accepts deposits, performs 1:1 swaps, processes withdrawals |

---

## Two Critical Safety Patterns

### 1. Debit Before Transfer (Withdraw)

When sending tokens **out** of the canister:

1. **Deduct** the user's internal balance first
2. **Then** perform the transfer on the token ledger

If reversed, a concurrent/reentering call could withdraw the same tokens twice (reentrancy attack). [[ICRC-2 Swap README](https://github.com/dfinity/examples/blob/master/motoko/icrc2-swap/README.md)]

### 2. Atomic Swap (No `await` Inside Swap Logic)

The swap function must exchange two users' balances **without any `await` calls**. On ICP, an `await` creates a commit point — if the function fails after an `await`, only the changes before it persist, leaving state inconsistent. [[ICRC-2 Swap README](https://github.com/dfinity/examples/blob/master/motoko/icrc2-swap/README.md)]

---

## Rust Skeleton (Based on ICP Rust CDK Patterns)

The knowledge sources do not provide a complete Rust swap canister, but based on the Rust CDK patterns documented, here is a structural skeleton consistent with the safety patterns described:

```rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::HashMap;
use candid::Principal;
use ic_cdk::call::{Call, CallResult};

// Internal balances for token_a and token_b per user
thread_local! {
    static BALANCES_A: RefCell<HashMap<Principal, u64>> = RefCell::new(HashMap::new());
    static BALANCES_B: RefCell<HashMap<Principal, u64>> = RefCell::new(HashMap::new());
}

// SAFETY PATTERN 2: No await inside swap — purely synchronous balance swap
#[ic_cdk::update]
fn swap(user_a: Principal, user_b: Principal) -> Result<(), String> {
    let bal_a = BALANCES_A.with(|b| *b.borrow().get(&user_a).unwrap_or(&0));
    let bal_b = BALANCES_B.with(|b| *b.borrow().get(&user_b).unwrap_or(&0));

    if bal_a == 0 || bal_b == 0 {
        return Err("InsufficientBalance".to_string());
    }

    // Atomic: all changes happen synchronously, no await
    BALANCES_A.with(|b| {
        let mut map = b.borrow_mut();
        map.insert(user_a, 0);
        map.insert(user_b, bal_a);
    });
    BALANCES_B.with(|b| {
        let mut map = b.borrow_mut();
        map.insert(user_b, 0);
        map.insert(user_a, bal_b);
    });

    Ok(())
}

// SAFETY PATTERN 1: Debit internal balance BEFORE calling the ledger
#[ic_cdk::update]
async fn withdraw(token_ledger: Principal, amount: u64) -> Result<(), String> {
    let caller = ic_cdk::caller();

    // Step 1: Debit first
    let current = BALANCES_A.with(|b| *b.borrow().get(&caller).unwrap_or(&0));
    if current < amount {
        return Err("InsufficientBalance".to_string());
    }
    BALANCES_A.with(|b| b.borrow_mut().insert(caller, current - amount));

    // Step 2: Then transfer on the ledger
    let result: CallResult<(Result<u64, String>,)> =
        Call::bounded_wait(token_ledger, "icrc1_transfer")
            .with_arg(&amount)
            .await;

    match result {
        Ok(_) => Ok(()),
        Err(e) => {
            // Rollback on failure
            BALANCES_A.with(|b| b.borrow_mut().insert(caller, current));
            Err(format!("Transfer failed: {:?}", e))
        }
    }
}

ic_cdk::export_candid!();
```

> **Note:** This is a simplified illustration. A production implementation requires proper ICRC-2 `transfer_from` calls, deposit handling, and full error management.

